How to Add Song Credits to Genius Lyrics
What are credits?
It is a list of the musicians involved in the creation of a song. They are divided into the following categories:
Primary Artists:
 The artist on whose behalf the song was released. Genius Credits must strictly match the data from streaming services, especially Apple Music. If there the main artist is represented by a list of several artists (as in "MotorSport" by Migos, Nicki Minaj & Cardi B), then you should group the names of all artists in the "Primary Artists" field into one record, then use the "Add Additional Credits" function to create the "Primary Artists" field and specify the name of each artist separately there.
The exception is "supergroups" - musical groups made up of already established artists. On streaming services, their names are often supplemented with the names of their members (for example, KIDS SEE GHOSTS, Kanye West & Kid Cudi). For simplicity's sake, leave just the band name.
Writers:
 In the writers field, list the people who contributed to the song: this includes both music and lyrics writers.
Featured Artists:
 Featured Artists are artists who made a significant contribution to the song. Usually with their vocals or co-writing.
Producers
: This refers to the producers of the music. In hip-hop, this is most often the person who wrote the beat.
Additional Credits:
 This section covers all the other professionals who contributed to the song: this includes musicians who played certain instruments, sound engineers, etc.
When should/should I add credits?
You can add data on Genius,
ONLY
 if they have been officially submitted. This means:
It is strictly forbidden to fill out credits based on your own speculation
Be prepared at any time to show a reputable source confirming your credits.
To avoid clogging up artist discographies, never fill out credits for the following types of pages:
Song translations
Fan mashups
Unofficial remixes
Unofficially released covers
Songs created with artificial intelligence (AI)
Where to find credits
Album Booklet:
 The booklet spreads of most physical media (CDs, vinyl records) contain credenzas. When purchasing a digital version of an album, such booklets are sometimes included electronically.
Copyright societies:
 These are organizations that help artists receive royalties for their musical works. In order for a copyright society to know who to pay money to, authors register their songs with the society's registry.
->
How to search for credits on copyright society websites
Streaming services:
 Streaming services such as Spotify, Apple Music, TIDAL
(and Yandex Music for Russian-speaking artists as well)
 store credits in their metadata.
->
How to search for credits on streaming services
Social Media:
 Information about songwriters is often provided by reputable media and even by the artists themselves in their social networks. It can be used as credits if the source is trustworthy.
Other sites:
 Professional music metadata collection sites like AllMusic or Discogs are fine as sources. Crowdsourcing sites like Wikipedia can be used as references, but information from there needs to be backed up with additional resources.
List of music professions and their accepted spelling
The same music profession may be referred to slightly differently in different sources, so we have developed our own list with musician names. When filling out the credits, please refer to it to choose the form of spelling accepted on our site. Please note that only frequently used credits are listed. If the credits you wish to specify are not added, please contact the
editors or moderators
 for assistance. When filling out the credits, remember that each role
should have a separate field.
For each of the music professions in the list below, the Russian translation is listed in parentheses. You do not need to provide a translation when filling out the data - just use the English title.
